1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Imagine you own this warehouse table. order_status is live.
Now you want to delete legacy_status. That sounds like one SQL statement—
until a model, dashboard, or Spark job still reads it.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:22,000
In our live scope test, repository search found one consumer.
DataHub field lineage found thirty-one, across seven complete pages.
The hard part is knowing what must move before DROP COLUMN.

3
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Retirement Conductor is the workflow around that deletion.
It finds consumers, changes only what is allowed, runs their real tests,
checks the graph again, and then deletes the old column—or stops.

4
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Here is one real campaign. Codex is the operator interface,
not the safety authority. Through DataHub and our MCP server,
it finds the exact dbt model and proposes a one-file change.

5
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Before changing code, it stops.
I review the file, validators, and digest, then authorize that exact plan
outside the agent. The agent cannot approve itself.

6
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:22,000
The same task applies the authorized change. The pull request changes one line.
dbt parse, build, test, and semantic checks pass.
The result is rechecked, published to DataHub, and read back.

7
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Migrating the consumer is not the finish line.
In a separate consequential run of the same protocol,
a privileged command checks everything and executes a real PostgreSQL DROP COLUMN.
The old field is gone, Spark succeeds, and replay is refused.

8
00:01:47,000 --> 00:02:17,000
After approval, an ad hoc Spark job appears in DataHub,
still reading legacy_status. The execution-time answer flips to unsafe.
The final command commits nothing, and the old column stays.
That is not dashboard theatre.

9
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:32,000
In the matched static-signoff control, nobody rechecks the graph.
The column is dropped, and Spark fails with SQL state 42703:
column does not exist. Retirement Conductor prevents that exact failure.

10
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:57,000
The Workbench makes the consequential campaign readable:
consumers, exact change, native tests, freshness, and why the decision changed.
DataHub is not decoration, and the model does not decide safety.
Retirement Conductor delivers a verified database change—or a safe refusal.
